==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|lunching||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|en|lunching}}, of uncertain origin.
Possibly related to {{m|en|lunch}}, q.v. It is unclear which came first. Possibly influenced by {{m|en|nuncheon|t=light snack taken in the afternoon}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈlʌntʃ.ən/|/ˈlʌnʃ.ən/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|lun|cheon}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-luncheon.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌntʃən|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[formal]] [[meal]] served in the middle of the day.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author=w:Margery Allingham|title=w:The China Governess
|chapter=2|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=Now that she had rested and had fed from the '''luncheon''' tray Mrs. Broome had just removed, she had reverted to her normal gaiety.&nbsp; She looked cool in a grey tailored cotton dress with a terracotta scarf and shoes and her hair a black silk helmet.}}
#* {{quote-av |1=en |year=1996 |author=Writing staff |actor=w:Harry Shearer |role=w:Principal Skinner |episode=w:22 Short Films About Springfield |title=w:The Simpsons |season=7 |number=21 |text=''Superintendent Chalmers:'' Well, Seymour, I made it — despite your directions.<br/>''Principal Skinner:'' Ahhh, Superintendent Chalmers! Welcome! I hope you're prepared for an unforgettable '''luncheon'''.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} Any midday meal; [[lunch]].
#* '''1931''', Mildred Wirt (as Carolyn Keene), ''The Mystery at Lilac Inn'' (page 4)
#*: "Have you had '''luncheon'''?"
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A lump of food.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A portion of food taken at any time except at a regular meal; an informal or light repast.
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|II
|passage=At twilight in the summer there is never anybody to fear—man, woman, or cat—in the chambers and at that hour the mice come out. They do not eat parchment or foolscap or red tape, but they eat the '''luncheon''' crumbs.}}

